      "status_notes": "CVE-2026-6100: lzma.LZMADecompressor, bz2.BZ2Decompressor and zlib decompression objects (used by gzip.GzipFile) could use freed memory when a decompression call failed with MemoryError and the object was reused. Upstream fixed it in https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/148395 (3.13 commit https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/c3cf71c3366fe49acb776a639405c0eea6169c20), first released in CPython 3.13.14; see https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-6100. The fix adds one store that clears the stream's input pointer on the error path of each of the _lzma, _bz2 and zlib extension modules. Compared the stores of zero to memory in the machine code of the _lzma, _bz2 and zlib modules in the standard library of every build published in the DHI Debian repository (shipped in libpython-3.13-stdlib up to 3.13.14-1 and in libpython-3.13-minimal from 3.13.14-2), 3.13.13-0 to 3.13.18-7 (26 builds), amd64 and arm64. On each architecture every build has the same stores into the decompressor objects as 3.13.14-0, which is built from CPython 3.13.14 and contains the upstream fix (from 3.13.15 the _bz2 module differs only in a stack slot, after a separate upstream bz2 change), and the zlib module's disassembly in the 3.13.13 builds is identical to that in 3.13.14-0. Every published build has the fix, so the range ends at 3.13.13-0, the lowest published build. The 3.13.13-0 to 3.13.13-2 libpython-3.13-stdlib packages are published as architecture all and carry the aarch64 build of these modules for both architectures, a packaging defect that does not change the result."
